{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XHXGNytm4Enjm4mUVVVgMa","type":"tool_result","content":"% For the practitioner -- drafted by loop0015 item 06.\n\\section{For the practitioner}\n\\label{sec:practitioner}\n\n\\placeholder{Item 06 drafts this section: a SAT solver and a model counter on random 3-CNF at $n = 20\\ldots 60$ against $2^{\\rhon}$ and $2^n$ (figure labelled \\texttt{fig:practitioner}, from \\repofile{paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}); the verified guarantee is never the binding one on any machine. Ends with what this does not claim''.}\n====\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The verified exponent runtimeScale(n) = rho(n), as a multiple of n.\n\nPaper 3, item 02 (Ralph loop0015, 2026-11-05).  The comparator statement of OpenAI's\nfamily 378 (lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean at commit 4eaee875, lines\n132-134) defines\n\n    runtimeScale n = n * (log (log (n + 16)))^2 / sqrt (log (n + 16))\n\nwith Real.log, the natural logarithm.  The verified time bound is\nC (L+1)^d 2^(A * runtimeScale n) (lines 136-139 and 210-211).  Brute force over all 2^n\nassignments is 2^n * poly.  This script prints rho(n)/n -- the verified exponent relative\nto n at A = 1 -- at a list of sizes, the two crossings of the level 1, and the peak; it\nwrites the table paper3/latex/tables/runtime_scale.tex and the figure\npaper3/latex/figures/runtime_scale.pdf (log-n axis from 10 to 10^2500, the line rho(n)/n,\nthe level 1, the practical range 50..10^6 shaded).\n\nEverything is computed in y = ln(n + 16): rho(n)/n = (ln y)^2 / sqrt(y), so no value of n\nis ever formed as a float beyond 10^300 (n = e^5504 is 10^2390).  Derived from\nreports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py; imports nothing from the clone.\n\nRegenerate:  python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py\n  -> stdout (the table and the landmarks), paper3/latex/tables/runtime_scale.tex,\n     paper3/latex/figures/runtime_scale.pdf and .png\nVerify the small-n values by hand: rho(n)/n = (ln ln(n+16))^2 / sqrt(ln(n+16)).\n\"\"\"\nimport math\nimport pathlib\nimport sys\n\nHERE = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\nFIG = HERE / \"latex\" / \"figures\"\nTAB = HERE / \"latex\" / \"tables\"\nFIG.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\nTAB.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\nLN10 = math.log(10.0)\n\n\ndef ratio_of_y(y: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"rho(n)/n as a function of y = ln(n + 16).\"\"\"\n    return math.log(y) ** 2 / math.sqrt(y)\n\n\ndef ratio_of_n(n: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"rho(n)/n for a size n small enough to be a float (n < 1e300).\"\"\"\n    return ratio_of_y(math.log(n + 16.0))\n\n\ndef ratio_of_log10n(l10: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"rho(n)/n for n = 10^l10; exact at small n, y = l10 ln 10 + ln(1 + 16/n) beyond float range.\"\"\"\n    if l10 < 290:\n        return ratio_of_n(10.0 ** l10)\n    return ratio_of_y(l10 * LN10)  # 16/n is below double precision here\n\n\ndef rho_of_n(n: float) -> float:\n    return n * ratio_of_n(n)\n\n\n# --- landmarks --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# (ln y)^2 / sqrt y is maximal where d/dy = 0: 2 ln y / y * sqrt y - (ln y)^2 / (2 sqrt y) = 0\n# -> ln y = 4 -> y = e^4, value 16 / e^2.\nPEAK_Y = math.e ** 4\nPEAK_VALUE = 16.0 / math.e ** 2\nPEAK_LOG10_N = PEAK_Y / LN10  # n = e^(e^4) - 16; the -16 is invisible at this size\n\n\ndef bisect(f, lo, hi, iters=200):\n    for _ in range(iters):\n        mid = 0.5 * (lo + hi)\n        if f(mid) > 0:\n            lo = mid\n        else:\n            hi = mid\n    return 0.5 * (lo + hi)\n\n\n# upper crossing: (ln y)^2 = sqrt y with y > e^4; f > 0 means ratio > 1 on the left\nUPPER_Y = bisect(lambda y: ratio_of_y(y) - 1.0, PEAK_Y, 1.0e6)\nUPPER_LOG10_N = UPPER_Y / LN10\n# lower crossing in n (integers): the first n with rho(n)/n >= 1\nLOWER_N = next(n for n in range(1, 10_000) if ratio_of_n(n) >= 1.0)\n\n\n# --- the table --------------------------------------------------------------------------\nROWS = [  # (label for the table, log10 n or None, n as float or None)\n    (\"1\", None, 1.0),\n    (\"20\", None, 20.0),\n    (\"49\", None, 49.0),\n    (\"50\", None, 50.0),\n    (\"100\", None, 100.0),\n    (\"125\", None, 125.0),\n    (\"10^3\", None, 1.0e3),\n    (\"10^6\", None, 1.0e6),\n    (\"10^{12}\", None, 1.0e12),\n    (\"10^{23.7}\", PEAK_LOG10_N, None),\n    (\"10^{100}\", 100.0, None),\n    (\"10^{1000}\", 1000.0, None),\n    (\"e^{5504} \\\\approx 10^{2390}\", UPPER_LOG10_N, None),\n    (\"10^{3000}\", 3000.0, None),\n]\n\n\ndef row_values(label, l10, n):\n    if n is not None:\n        r = ratio_of_n(n)\n        return label, r, (rho_of_n(n) if n < 1e300 else None)\n    return label, ratio_of_log10n(l10), None\n\n\nprint(\"verified exponent rho(n) = n (ln ln(n+16))^2 / sqrt(ln(n+16)), comparator lines 132-134\")\nprint(f\"{'n':>28}  {'rho(n)/n':>10}  {'rho(n)':>14}\")\ntable_rows = []\nfor label, l10, n in ROWS:\n    lab, r, rho = row_values(label, l10, n)\n    rho_s = f\"{rho:14.4g}\" if rho is not None else f\"{'':>14}\"\n    print(f\"{lab:>28}  {r:10.4f}  {rho_s}\")\n    table_rows.append((lab, r, rho))\n\nprint()\nprint(f\"first n with rho(n)/n >= 1: n = {LOWER_N}  \"\n      f\"(rho({LOWER_N - 1})/{LOWER_N - 1} = {ratio_of_n(LOWER_N - 1):.4f}, \"\n      f\"rho({LOWER_N})/{LOWER_N} = {ratio_of_n(LOWER_N):.4f})\")\nprint(f\"peak: ratio 16/e^2 = {PEAK_VALUE:.4f} at ln(n+16) = e^4 = {PEAK_Y:.4f}, \"\n      f\"i.e. n ~ 10^{PEAK_LOG10_N:.2f}\")\nprint(f\"back below 1: ln(n+16) = {UPPER_Y:.2f}, i.e. n ~ e^{UPPER_Y:.0f} ~ 10^{UPPER_LOG10_N:.0f}\")\nprint(f\"check: ratio at ln(n+16) = {UPPER_Y - 1:.0f}: {ratio_of_y(UPPER_Y - 1):.6f}; \"\n      f\"at {UPPER_Y + 1:.0f}: {ratio_of_y(UPPER_Y + 1):.6f}\")\n\n# --- the LaTeX table --------------------------------------------------------------------\nwith open(TAB / \"runtime_scale.tex\", \"w\") as fh:\n    fh.write(\"% Generated by paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py (python3 -I); do not edit.\\n\")\n    fh.write(\"% rho(n) = n (ln ln(n+16))^2 / sqrt(ln(n+16)), the comparator's runtimeScale.\\n\")\n    fh.write(\"\\\\begin{tabular}{@{}rrr@{}}\\n\\\\toprule\\n\")\n    fh.write(\"$n$ & $\\\\rhon/n$ & $\\\\rhon$ \\\\\\\\\\n\\\\midrule\\n\")\n    for lab, r, rho in table_rows:\n        if rho is None:\n            rho_s = \"---\"\n        elif rho < 1e4:\n            rho_s = f\"{rho:.2f}\"\n        else:\n            e = int(math.floor(math.log10(rho)))\n            rho_s = f\"${rho / 10 ** e:.2f}\\\\times 10^{{{e}}}$\"\n        fh.write(f\"${lab}$ & {r:.4f} & {rho_s} \\\\\\\\\\n\")\n    fh.write(\"\\\\bottomrule\\n\\\\end{tabular}\\n\")\nprint(\"written\", TAB / \"runtime_scale.tex\")\n\n# --- the figure -------------------------------------------------------------------------\ntry:\n    import numpy as np\n    import matplotlib\n    matplotlib.use(\"Agg\")\n    import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nexcept ImportError as exc:  # the table and the numbers above do not need it\n    print(\"figure skipped:\", exc)\n    sys.exit(0)\n\n# palette: dataviz reference categorical slot 1 and neutrals (validated, light mode)\nBLUE, INK, INK2, GRID, SHADE = \"#2a78d6\", \"#0b0b0b\", \"#52514e\", \"#d9d8d3\", \"#f1e7d8\"\nplt.rcParams.update({\"font.family\": \"serif\", \"font.size\": 9, \"axes.edgecolor\": INK2,\n                     \"axes.labelcolor\": INK, \"xtick.color\": INK2, \"ytick.color\": INK2,\n                     \"text.color\": INK})\n\n# dense near the small-n crossing, then even in log10 n up to 10^2500\nL = np.concatenate([np.linspace(1.0, 7.0, 600), np.linspace(7.0, 2500.0, 2500)])\nV = [ratio_of_log10n(float(l)) for l in L]\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6.4, 3.3), dpi=200)\nax.set_facecolor(\"#fcfcfb\")\nfig.patch.set_facecolor(\"white\")\nax.axvspan(math.log10(50), 6.0, color=SHADE, zorder=0, lw=0)\nax.axhline(1.0, color=INK2, lw=1.0, ls=(0, (4, 3)), zorder=1)\nax.plot(L, V, color=BLUE, lw=2.0, zorder=3)\nax.set_xscale(\"log\")\nax.set_xlim(1.0, 2500.0)\nax.set_ylim(0.0, 2.4)\nticks = [1, 2, 3, 6, 12, 24, 100, 1000, 2500]\nax.set_xticks(ticks)\nax.set_xticklabels([\"$10$\", \"$10^2$\", \"$10^3$\", \"$10^6$\", \"$10^{12}$\", \"$10^{24}$\",\n                    \"$10^{100}$\", \"$10^{1000}$\", \"$10^{2500}$\"])\nax.set_xlabel(\"number of variables $n$ (log scale in $\\\\log_{10} n$)\")\nax.set_ylabel(\"$\\\\rho(n)/n$: verified exponent over $n$\\n(bound $= 2^{A\\\\rho(n)}\\\\cdot$poly, drawn at $A=1$)\")\n# landmarks\nax.scatter([math.log10(LOWER_N)], [ratio_of_n(LOWER_N)], s=28, color=\"white\", edgecolor=BLUE, linewidth=1.6, zorder=4)\nax.annotate(f\"$n = {LOWER_N}$: first above 1\", (math.log10(LOWER_N), ratio_of_n(LOWER_N)), (1.08, 0.55),\n            fontsize=8, color=INK, arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=\"-\", color=INK2, lw=0.8))\nax.scatter([PEAK_LOG10_N], [PEAK_VALUE], s=28, color=\"white\", edgecolor=BLUE, linewidth=1.6, zorder=4)\nax.annotate(f\"peak $16/e^2 = {PEAK_VALUE:.2f}$ at $n \\\\approx 10^{{{PEAK_LOG10_N:.1f}}}$\",\n            (PEAK_LOG10_N, PEAK_VALUE), (45, 2.22), fontsize=8, color=INK,\n            arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=\"-\", color=INK2, lw=0.8))\nax.scatter([UPPER_LOG10_N], [1.0], s=28, color=\"white\", edgecolor=BLUE, linewidth=1.6, zorder=4)\nax.annotate(f\"below 1 again at $n \\\\approx e^{{{UPPER_Y:.0f}}} \\\\approx 10^{{{UPPER_LOG10_N:.0f}}}$\",\n            (UPPER_LOG10_N, 1.0), (700, 0.55), fontsize=8, color=INK, ha=\"center\",\n            arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=\"-\", color=INK2, lw=0.8))\nax.text(math.sqrt(math.log10(50) * 6.0), 0.12, \"practical range\\n$50 \\\\leq n \\\\leq 10^6$\", fontsize=8,\n        color=INK2, ha=\"center\", va=\"bottom\")\nax.text(14, 1.03, \"brute force: $2^n$\", color=INK2, fontsize=8.2, ha=\"left\", va=\"bottom\")\nax.grid(True, color=GRID, lw=0.6, which=\"major\")\nax.set_axisbelow(True)\nfor s in (\"top\", \"right\"):\n    ax.spines[s].set_visible(False)\nfig.tight_layout()\nfig.savefig(FIG / \"runtime_scale.pdf\")\nfig.savefig(FIG / \"runtime_scale.png\")\nprint(\"written\", FIG / \"runtime_scale.pdf\")\n====\n# ETH_AMBIGUOUS — questions the loop decided for itself, for the owner\n\n**Protocol** (owner, 2026-11-05). Paper 3 is written by Ralph loop0015\nwith no stopping midway. Whenever an item finds something ambiguous — a\nwording, a claim, a citation form, a choice between two sound ways of\ndoing a thing — it **decides**, writes **one entry here per question**,\nand continues. Each entry has three parts: *what was ambiguous*, *what\nthe item chose*, *what the owner may want to change*. Entries are dated\nand numbered (`S` for setup, then the item number and a letter: `01a`,\n`01b`, …). Nothing here changes the title, which is fixed:\n\n> Exponential time hypothesis is false... but the verified algorithm is slower than trying all 2^n assignments at every practical size.\n\nItem 09 lists in the paper's conclusion the entries that affect the text;\nitem 11 lists all of them in `README.md`. The owner resolves an entry by\nediting the paper and striking the entry (or by leaving it, which is also\nan answer).\n\n## Entries\n\n### S1 — the author line (setup, 2026-11-05)\n- **Ambiguous**: who the authors are. Paper 1 is Linhares and Yanasse;\n  the brief for this paper names \"Alexandre Linhares\" and \"the MOSP\n  project\" as a placeholder.\n- **Chosen**: `\\author{Alexandre Linhares \\and the MOSP project}` in\n  `latex/main.tex`, with a comment marking the second author as a\n  placeholder; the acknowledgements will carry the AI-use disclosure in\n  paper 1's form.\n- **Owner may change**: the second author (Yanasse? none? a project\n  name?), affiliations, ORCID.\n\n### S2 — `2^n` in the typeset title (setup, 2026-11-05)\n- **Ambiguous**: the title string carries the plain-text `2^n`; LaTeX\n  cannot typeset `^` in text mode, and a typeset title would ordinarily\n  show a superscript.\n- **Chosen**: `\\title{... all $2^n$ assignments ...}` (a superscript `n`\n  in the PDF) with `\\ldots` for the ellipsis; the PDF metadata\n  (`pdftitle`) carries the plain string with the caret literal (`2\\string^n`, since hyperref drops a bare `^` from PDF strings); the plain\n  string also sits in a comment directly above `\\title`. The gate strips\n  `$` and `\\ldots` before comparing. Nothing else in the title is\n  touched.\n- **Owner may change**: typeset the caret literally (`2\\textasciicircum\n  n`) if the plain form is wanted on the page.\n\n### S3 — where the companion Lean lives (setup, 2026-11-05)\n- **Ambiguous**: the restatement `SubexpCount` and the verbatim machine\n  model are under `lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/`, a path\n  named for loop0014's pathwidth question, which this paper does not\n  touch. Cite them there, or move them to a neutral path?\n- **Chosen**: item 07 moves them — `PrimitiveMachine.lean` and the\n  ETH-only declarations of `Frame.lean` (`SubexpCount`,\n  `ExactCountAndSat`, `SubexpCount.anti`, `ExactCountAndSat.subexpCount`,\n  `runtimeBound_eq`, `tendsto_log_sq_div_sqrt`, `runtimeScale_isLittleO`)\n  — to `lean/MOSPFormalization/ETH/{PrimitiveMachine,Statement}.lean`;\n  `SubexpPathwidth/Frame.lean` imports `ETH.Statement` and keeps its\n  pathwidth side. loop0014's report will then name the old path for\n  those declarations; a one-line note in `reports/subexp_pathwidth.md`\n  §2.1 is the smallest fix and item 07 adds it.\n- **Owner may change**: keep them in place and cite the old path; or a\n  different directory name.\n\n### S4 — Bonnet's venue year, and the width references dropped (setup, 2026-11-05)\n- **Ambiguous**: the original brief listed Lokshtanov–Marx–Saurabh,\n  Cygan et al. (Cut & Count), Lampis 2025 and Bonnet 2025 for `refs.bib`;\n  the owner's scope change the same day removed every width-parameter\n  consequence from the paper, so none of the four is cited. (For the\n  record, Crossref on 2026-11-05 gives Bonnet as STOC 2025,\n  `10.1145/3717823.3718117`, and *J. ACM* issued 2026-10-10,\n  `10.1145/3833387`; loop0014's report says \"*J. ACM* 2025\".)\n- **Chosen**: the four entries are not in `refs.bib`; `refs.bib` carries\n  the OpenAI manuscript, Impagliazzo–Paturi 2001, Impagliazzo–Paturi–Zane\n  2001, the human-audit paper arXiv 2608.14673, and `@misc` entries for\n  the comparator and nanoda repositories (URLs; item 08 fills what it\n  fetches).\n- **Owner may change**: nothing unless a width reference is wanted back.\n\n### S5 — per-item cap in the driver (setup, 2026-11-05)\n- **Ambiguous**: the loop0014 driver has one global cap; the replay\n  needs 8 h and every other item 4 h.\n- **Chosen**: `loop.py` reads `[cap N h]` from an item's first line\n====\n347:\\paragraph{What a transferred algorithm inherits.}\n348-The manuscript's one positive transfer is Set Splitting with sets of\n349-size at most $k$ (p.~5, lines 239--243): one variable per universe\n350-element and two clauses per set give a $k$-CNF with ``one variable per\n351-universe element, so for fixed $k$ Theorem 1.1 gives subexponential\n352-time in the universe size, up to a polynomial input-size factor,''\n353-citing \\citet{Cygan2016}'s Observation~3.6. Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}\n354-tags this supported: the encoding is on paper, the counter is the\n355-verified one. What such a transfer inherits is the verified guarantee\n356-on the SAT instance it builds, $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rho(n')}$ with $n'$ the\n357-number of SAT variables, and therefore the title's limitation with it:\n358-at $A = 1$ the exponential factor is above $2^{n'}$ for every\n359-$50 \\le n' < e^{5504}$ (Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}), with $C, A, d$\n360-unknown. In general terms: an algorithm obtained from the verified\n361-statement through a variable-controlling reduction is a $2^{o(n')}$\n362-algorithm whose verified guarantee is weaker, at every practical size,\n363-than trying every assignment of the SAT instance it constructs. That is\n364-not a defect of the transfer; it is the same sentence as the title,\n365-read through a reduction.\n366-\n367-\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n368-\\label{sec:consequences-not}\n369-\n370-\\begin{itemize}\n371-\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm'' is\n372-  refuted}, for any problem, any parameter or any constant $c$. Such a\n373-  statement, proved from SETH, has lost its proof and kept its truth\n374-  value, which is unknown.\n375-\\item \\textbf{No statement ``no $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm for $X$'' is\n376-  refuted} either, unless $X$ has a reduction to fixed-width SAT that\n377-  controls the number of variables; the manuscript names one such","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"56304e7d-c5a9-41ea-80a7-c4a817bd6f76","uuid":"010ced45-37b6-4c86-b171-d4e7be196242","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:35:35.721Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"% For the practitioner -- drafted by loop0015 item 06.\n\\section{For the practitioner}\n\\label{sec:practitioner}\n\n\\placeholder{Item 06 drafts this section: a SAT solver and a model counter on random 3-CNF at $n = 20\\ldots 60$ against $2^{\\rhon}$ and $2^n$ (figure labelled \\texttt{fig:practitioner}, from \\repofile{paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}); the verified guarantee is never the binding one on any machine. Ends with what this does not claim''.}\n====\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The verified exponent runtimeScale(n) = rho(n), as a multiple of n.\n\nPaper 3, item 02 (Ralph loop0015, 2026-11-05).  The comparator statement of OpenAI's\nfamily 378 (lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean at commit 4eaee875, lines\n132-134) defines\n\n    runtimeScale n = n * (log (log (n + 16)))^2 / sqrt (log (n + 16))\n\nwith Real.log, the natural logarithm.  The verified time bound is\nC (L+1)^d 2^(A * runtimeScale n) (lines 136-139 and 210-211).  Brute force over all 2^n\nassignments is 2^n * poly.  This script prints rho(n)/n -- the verified exponent relative\nto n at A = 1 -- at a list of sizes, the two crossings of the level 1, and the peak; it\nwrites the table paper3/latex/tables/runtime_scale.tex and the figure\npaper3/latex/figures/runtime_scale.pdf (log-n axis from 10 to 10^2500, the line rho(n)/n,\nthe level 1, the practical range 50..10^6 shaded).\n\nEverything is computed in y = ln(n + 16): rho(n)/n = (ln y)^2 / sqrt(y), so no value of n\nis ever formed as a float beyond 10^300 (n = e^5504 is 10^2390).  Derived from\nreports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py; imports nothing from the clone.\n\nRegenerate:  python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py\n  -> stdout (the table and the landmarks), paper3/latex/tables/runtime_scale.tex,\n     paper3/latex/figures/runtime_scale.pdf and .png\nVerify the small-n values by hand: rho(n)/n = (ln ln(n+16))^2 / sqrt(ln(n+16)).\n\"\"\"\nimport math\nimport pathlib\nimport sys\n\nHERE = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\nFIG = HERE / \"latex\" / \"figures\"\nTAB = HERE / \"latex\" / \"tables\"\nFIG.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\nTAB.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\nLN10 = math.log(10.0)\n\n\ndef ratio_of_y(y: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"rho(n)/n as a function of y = ln(n + 16).\"\"\"\n    return math.log(y) ** 2 / math.sqrt(y)\n\n\ndef ratio_of_n(n: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"rho(n)/n for a size n small enough to be a float (n < 1e300).\"\"\"\n    return ratio_of_y(math.log(n + 16.0))\n\n\ndef ratio_of_log10n(l10: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"rho(n)/n for n = 10^l10; exact at small n, y = l10 ln 10 + ln(1 + 16/n) beyond float range.\"\"\"\n    if l10 < 290:\n        return ratio_of_n(10.0 ** l10)\n    return ratio_of_y(l10 * LN10)  # 16/n is below double precision here\n\n\ndef rho_of_n(n: float) -> float:\n    return n * ratio_of_n(n)\n\n\n# --- landmarks --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# (ln y)^2 / sqrt y is maximal where d/dy = 0: 2 ln y / y * sqrt y - (ln y)^2 / (2 sqrt y) = 0\n# -> ln y = 4 -> y = e^4, value 16 / e^2.\nPEAK_Y = math.e ** 4\nPEAK_VALUE = 16.0 / math.e ** 2\nPEAK_LOG10_N = PEAK_Y / LN10  # n = e^(e^4) - 16; the -16 is invisible at this size\n\n\ndef bisect(f, lo, hi, iters=200):\n    for _ in range(iters):\n        mid = 0.5 * (lo + hi)\n        if f(mid) > 0:\n            lo = mid\n        else:\n            hi = mid\n    return 0.5 * (lo + hi)\n\n\n# upper crossing: (ln y)^2 = sqrt y with y > e^4; f > 0 means ratio > 1 on the left\nUPPER_Y = bisect(lambda y: ratio_of_y(y) - 1.0, PEAK_Y, 1.0e6)\nUPPER_LOG10_N = UPPER_Y / LN10\n# lower crossing in n (integers): the first n with rho(n)/n >= 1\nLOWER_N = next(n for n in range(1, 10_000) if ratio_of_n(n) >= 1.0)\n\n\n# --- the table --------------------------------------------------------------------------\nROWS = [  # (label for the table, log10 n or None, n as float or None)\n    (\"1\", None, 1.0),\n    (\"20\", None, 20.0),\n    (\"49\", None, 49.0),\n    (\"50\", None, 50.0),\n    (\"100\", None, 100.0),\n    (\"125\", None, 125.0),\n    (\"10^3\", None, 1.0e3),\n    (\"10^6\", None, 1.0e6),\n    (\"10^{12}\", None, 1.0e12),\n    (\"10^{23.7}\", PEAK_LOG10_N, None),\n    (\"10^{100}\", 100.0, None),\n    (\"10^{1000}\", 1000.0, None),\n    (\"e^{5504} \\\\approx 10^{2390}\", UPPER_LOG10_N, None),\n    (\"10^{3000}\", 3000.0, None),\n]\n\n\ndef row_values(label, l10, n):\n    if n is not None:\n        r = ratio_of_n(n)\n        return label, r, (rho_of_n(n) if n < 1e300 else None)\n    return label, ratio_of_log10n(l10), None\n\n\nprint(\"verified exponent rho(n) = n (ln ln(n+16))^2 / sqrt(ln(n+16)), comparator lines 132-134\")\nprint(f\"{'n':>28}  {'rho(n)/n':>10}  {'rho(n)':>14}\")\ntable_rows = []\nfor label, l10, n in ROWS:\n    lab, r, rho = row_values(label, l10, n)\n    rho_s = f\"{rho:14.4g}\" if rho is not None else f\"{'':>14}\"\n    print(f\"{lab:>28}  {r:10.4f}  {rho_s}\")\n    table_rows.append((lab, r, rho))\n\nprint()\nprint(f\"first n with rho(n)/n >= 1: n = {LOWER_N}  \"\n      f\"(rho({LOWER_N - 1})/{LOWER_N - 1} = {ratio_of_n(LOWER_N - 1):.4f}, \"\n      f\"rho({LOWER_N})/{LOWER_N} = {ratio_of_n(LOWER_N):.4f})\")\nprint(f\"peak: ratio 16/e^2 = {PEAK_VALUE:.4f} at ln(n+16) = e^4 = {PEAK_Y:.4f}, \"\n      f\"i.e. n ~ 10^{PEAK_LOG10_N:.2f}\")\nprint(f\"back below 1: ln(n+16) = {UPPER_Y:.2f}, i.e. n ~ e^{UPPER_Y:.0f} ~ 10^{UPPER_LOG10_N:.0f}\")\nprint(f\"check: ratio at ln(n+16) = {UPPER_Y - 1:.0f}: {ratio_of_y(UPPER_Y - 1):.6f}; \"\n      f\"at {UPPER_Y + 1:.0f}: {ratio_of_y(UPPER_Y + 1):.6f}\")\n\n# --- the LaTeX table --------------------------------------------------------------------\nwith open(TAB / \"runtime_scale.tex\", \"w\") as fh:\n    fh.write(\"% Generated by paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py (python3 -I); do not edit.\\n\")\n    fh.write(\"% rho(n) = n (ln ln(n+16))^2 / sqrt(ln(n+16)), the comparator's runtimeScale.\\n\")\n    fh.write(\"\\\\begin{tabular}{@{}rrr@{}}\\n\\\\toprule\\n\")\n    fh.write(\"$n$ & $\\\\rhon/n$ & $\\\\rhon$ \\\\\\\\\\n\\\\midrule\\n\")\n    for lab, r, rho in table_rows:\n        if rho is None:\n            rho_s = \"---\"\n        elif rho < 1e4:\n            rho_s = f\"{rho:.2f}\"\n        else:\n            e = int(math.floor(math.log10(rho)))\n            rho_s = f\"${rho / 10 ** e:.2f}\\\\times 10^{{{e}}}$\"\n        fh.write(f\"${lab}$ & {r:.4f} & {rho_s} \\\\\\\\\\n\")\n    fh.write(\"\\\\bottomrule\\n\\\\end{tabular}\\n\")\nprint(\"written\", TAB / \"runtime_scale.tex\")\n\n# --- the figure -------------------------------------------------------------------------\ntry:\n    import numpy as np\n    import matplotlib\n    matplotlib.use(\"Agg\")\n    import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nexcept ImportError as exc:  # the table and the numbers above do not need it\n    print(\"figure skipped:\", exc)\n    sys.exit(0)\n\n# palette: dataviz reference categorical slot 1 and neutrals (validated, light mode)\nBLUE, INK, INK2, GRID, SHADE = \"#2a78d6\", \"#0b0b0b\", \"#52514e\", \"#d9d8d3\", \"#f1e7d8\"\nplt.rcParams.update({\"font.family\": \"serif\", \"font.size\": 9, \"axes.edgecolor\": INK2,\n                     \"axes.labelcolor\": INK, \"xtick.color\": INK2, \"ytick.color\": INK2,\n                     \"text.color\": INK})\n\n# dense near the small-n crossing, then even in log10 n up to 10^2500\nL = np.concatenate([np.linspace(1.0, 7.0, 600), np.linspace(7.0, 2500.0, 2500)])\nV = [ratio_of_log10n(float(l)) for l in L]\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6.4, 3.3), dpi=200)\nax.set_facecolor(\"#fcfcfb\")\nfig.patch.set_facecolor(\"white\")\nax.axvspan(math.log10(50), 6.0, color=SHADE, zorder=0, lw=0)\nax.axhline(1.0, color=INK2, lw=1.0, ls=(0, (4, 3)), zorder=1)\nax.plot(L, V, color=BLUE, lw=2.0, zorder=3)\nax.set_xscale(\"log\")\nax.set_xlim(1.0, 2500.0)\nax.set_ylim(0.0, 2.4)\nticks = [1, 2, 3, 6, 12, 24, 100, 1000, 2500]\nax.set_xticks(ticks)\nax.set_xticklabels([\"$10$\", \"$10^2$\", \"$10^3$\", \"$10^6$\", \"$10^{12}$\", \"$10^{24}$\",\n                    \"$10^{100}$\", \"$10^{1000}$\", \"$10^{2500}$\"])\nax.set_xlabel(\"number of variables $n$ (log scale in $\\\\log_{10} n$)\")\nax.set_ylabel(\"$\\\\rho(n)/n$: verified exponent over $n$\\n(bound $= 2^{A\\\\rho(n)}\\\\cdot$poly, drawn at $A=1$)\")\n# landmarks\nax.scatter([math.log10(LOWER_N)], [ratio_of_n(LOWER_N)], s=28, color=\"white\", edgecolor=BLUE, linewidth=1.6, zorder=4)\nax.annotate(f\"$n = {LOWER_N}$: first above 1\", (math.log10(LOWER_N), ratio_of_n(LOWER_N)), (1.08, 0.55),\n            fontsize=8, color=INK, arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=\"-\", color=INK2, lw=0.8))\nax.scatter([PEAK_LOG10_N], [PEAK_VALUE], s=28, color=\"white\", edgecolor=BLUE, linewidth=1.6, zorder=4)\nax.annotate(f\"peak $16/e^2 = {PEAK_VALUE:.2f}$ at $n \\\\approx 10^{{{PEAK_LOG10_N:.1f}}}$\",\n            (PEAK_LOG10_N, PEAK_VALUE), (45, 2.22), fontsize=8, color=INK,\n            arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=\"-\", color=INK2, lw=0.8))\nax.scatter([UPPER_LOG10_N], [1.0], s=28, color=\"white\", edgecolor=BLUE, linewidth=1.6, zorder=4)\nax.annotate(f\"below 1 again at $n \\\\approx e^{{{UPPER_Y:.0f}}} \\\\approx 10^{{{UPPER_LOG10_N:.0f}}}$\",\n            (UPPER_LOG10_N, 1.0), (700, 0.55), fontsize=8, color=INK, ha=\"center\",\n            arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=\"-\", color=INK2, lw=0.8))\nax.text(math.sqrt(math.log10(50) * 6.0), 0.12, \"practical range\\n$50 \\\\leq n \\\\leq 10^6$\", fontsize=8,\n        color=INK2, ha=\"center\", va=\"bottom\")\nax.text(14, 1.03, \"brute force: $2^n$\", color=INK2, fontsize=8.2, ha=\"left\", va=\"bottom\")\nax.grid(True, color=GRID, lw=0.6, which=\"major\")\nax.set_axisbelow(True)\nfor s in (\"top\", \"right\"):\n    ax.spines[s].set_visible(False)\nfig.tight_layout()\nfig.savefig(FIG / \"runtime_scale.pdf\")\nfig.savefig(FIG / \"runtime_scale.png\")\nprint(\"written\", FIG / \"runtime_scale.pdf\")\n====\n# ETH_AMBIGUOUS — questions the loop decided for itself, for the owner\n\n**Protocol** (owner, 2026-11-05). Paper 3 is written by Ralph loop0015\nwith no stopping midway. Whenever an item finds something ambiguous — a\nwording, a claim, a citation form, a choice between two sound ways of\ndoing a thing — it **decides**, writes **one entry here per question**,\nand continues. Each entry has three parts: *what was ambiguous*, *what\nthe item chose*, *what the owner may want to change*. Entries are dated\nand numbered (`S` for setup, then the item number and a letter: `01a`,\n`01b`, …). Nothing here changes the title, which is fixed:\n\n> Exponential time hypothesis is false... but the verified algorithm is slower than trying all 2^n assignments at every practical size.\n\nItem 09 lists in the paper's conclusion the entries that affect the text;\nitem 11 lists all of them in `README.md`. The owner resolves an entry by\nediting the paper and striking the entry (or by leaving it, which is also\nan answer).\n\n## Entries\n\n### S1 — the author line (setup, 2026-11-05)\n- **Ambiguous**: who the authors are. Paper 1 is Linhares and Yanasse;\n  the brief for this paper names \"Alexandre Linhares\" and \"the MOSP\n  project\" as a placeholder.\n- **Chosen**: `\\author{Alexandre Linhares \\and