ection{What follows, in general terms}\n\\label{sec:consequences}\n\nSections~\\ref{sec:statement}--\\ref{sec:replay} read the verified statement,\ndrew the line between it and the manuscript, inspected the proof and\nreplayed it. This section asks what follows from the verified statement\nalone: which hypotheses it refutes and by what step, and what a lower\nbound that was derived from one of those hypotheses has lost. The\nmanuscript answers both questions itself, in \\S1.1.1--\\S1.1.2 (p.~4--5)\nand \\S1.1.3 (p.~5), and we quote it; what we add is the quantifier form\nof each hypothesis, the one line of the comparator statement that negates\nit, and the distinction, stated in general terms, between a theorem whose\npremise has become false and a statement that has been refuted. Nothing\nhere names a problem the manuscript's \\S1.1.1 and \\S1.1.3 do not name, and\nnothing here is about any problem's complexity beyond what those two\nsections state.\n\n\\subsection{The one property every refutation uses}\n\\label{sec:consequences-property}\n\nEvery refutation below rests on the same conjuncts of\n\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (comparator file lines 194--217,\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement}): for each fixed $k \\ge 2$ there is one\ndeterministic machine which, on the canonical encoding of any\n\\texttt{WidthAtMost}~$k$ formula $F$ on $n$ variables (line 38), halts\nwith \\texttt{countWord (count F)} on its count tape (lines 203--204, 147),\nthe bit $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$ on its answer tape and in its halting\nstate (lines 205--207), within $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ steps (lines\n210--211). Four features of that property do the work.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_016VzXLcXSRcqK7o66iLuSMC","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
