{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgjAjRW7fj1A6hPoLNaAN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GbR3xFfkCN4KqWj6F5qJEF","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat > paper3/latex/sec7_practitioner.tex <<'EOF'\n% For the practitioner -- drafted by loop0015 item 06 (2026-11-05).\n% Every number of this section is produced by\n%   nice -n 19 python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py --regenerate   (the measurement)\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py                           (table, figure, stdout)\n% from random 3-CNF the script generates; no MOSP or pathwidth instance, nothing\n% under solutions/. Timings are from one machine on one day with named\n% versions (tables/practitioner_meta.json). The two guarantee curves are shapes\n% at constant 1, and nothing here is a running time of the verified machine.\n\\section{For the practitioner}\n\\label{sec:practitioner}\n\nThe title compares two guarantees: the verified one,\n$C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ with $C, A, d$ existential (comparator file lines\n136--139 and 210--211, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), and the trivial one,\n$2^n \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ for trying every assignment. Neither is what\na practitioner runs. What runs on a machine, for the decision bit, is a\nconflict-driven clause-learning (CDCL) solver, and for the count an exact\nor approximate model counter; and no implementation of the verified\nmachine exists to run beside them. This section measures the tools a\npractitioner has, on random 3-CNF, and draws the two guarantees next to\nthe measurements as shapes, so that the reader can see where each sits.\nThe point is small and the section says it once: the verified guarantee\nis not the binding one on any machine, because it is above $2^n$ for\nevery $n \\ge 50$ (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}) while the solvers are many\norders of magnitude below $2^n$ at every size they were run at.\n\n\\subsection{Why the comparison is between guarantees and measurements}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-why}\n\nThe verified statement is existential in the machine (comparator line\n195: \\texttt{\\textexclamdown}\\ldots\\ \\emph{see below}) and the solution's\n3,977 files contain no \\texttt{\\#eval}, no \\texttt{def main} and no\n\\texttt{IO} action (checked read-only by the script, which reads every\nfile of the family with \\texttt{open()} and counts those three patterns:\n0, 0, 0). The library proves that a machine with the stated bound\nexists and exhibits one as a Lean term inside a development with 1,048\n\\texttt{noncomputable} declarations and no executable interface\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}); nothing in the clone runs it on an input, and\nwe found no implementation elsewhere. So the verified algorithm's actual\nrunning time on any formula is unknown, in both directions: nothing says\nit is slow, and nothing says it is fast. What is known is its\n\\emph{guarantee}, and a guarantee is compared with other guarantees. The\nmeasurements below are of other programs entirely; they are in the\nsection because the question a practitioner asks is not ``which\nguarantee is smaller'' but ``what would I run'', and the answer to that\nhas not changed.\n\n\\subsection{What was run}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-setup}\n\n\\paragraph{Instances.} Random 3-CNF on $n$ variables with\n$m = \\lfloor 4.26\\,n \\rceil$ clauses, each clause three distinct variables\nwith uniform signs, generated by the script from $(n, \\mathrm{seed})$;\ntwenty seeds per size; $n = 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 80, 100, 150, 200, 250,\n300, 400, 500$. The ratio $4.26$ is the empirical satisfiability\nthreshold of random 3-SAT \\citep{Selman1996Hard}, where decision is\nhardest for a given $n$ among random formulas (the cavity method puts the\nthreshold at $4.2667$, \\citealp{Mertens2006Threshold}); the sizes are\nchosen so that the smallest ones are where the title's crossing is\n($n = 50$) and the largest are where a CDCL solver on random formulas\nbegins to take seconds. These are random instances only; nothing is\nclaimed about structured formulas.\n\n\\paragraph{Tools.} CaDiCaL 1.9.5 \\citep{Biere2024CaDiCaL} through\npython-sat 1.9.dev15 \\citep{Ignatiev2018PySAT}, one \\texttt{solve()} call,\nfor the decision bit $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$; Ganak 2.8.0\n\\citep{Sharma2019Ganak}, through the pyganak binding (which runs its Arjun\npreprocessing and then the counter), for the exact count; ApproxMC 4.4.0\n\\citep{Chakraborty2013ApproxMC, Soos2019BIRD} at its defaults\n$\\varepsilon = 0.8$, $\\delta = 0.2$ with the projection set equal to all\nvariables, for an approximate count; and, at $n \\le 30$ only, exact\ncounting by enumeration over CaDiCaL with a blocking clause per model,\nwhich is not a counter at any larger size (it is linear in the count,\nand the count is exponential in $n$) and is here as the independent check\nof the two counters where it can reach. Ganak and ApproxMC were installed\nfrom PyPI for this section; no \\texttt{sharpSAT}, \\texttt{d4} or other\ncounter binary is on the host.\n\n\\paragraph{Protocol.} Each call runs in its own interpreter under\n\\texttt{nice -n 19}, at most eight at a time on the thirty-two cores of\nan AMD Ryzen 9 9950X (Linux 7.0.0, Python 3.12.13; the machine was shared\nwith other work, load average at the start in\n\\repofile{tables/practitioner_meta.json}), with a wall-clock budget of\n60~s per call; the time recorded is \\texttt{perf\\_counter} around the\nconstruction of the solver object and the one call, inside the child,\nwith the interpreter's start-up and the import outside it. A call over\nbudget is killed and recorded as censored; a size at which a tool finishes\nfewer than ten of its twenty instances ends that tool's escalation. The\nwhole run took WALLMIN minutes.\n\n\\subsection{What came out}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-results}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:practitioner} gives, per size, the number of satisfiable\ninstances, CaDiCaL's median and maximum over the twenty seeds, the number\nof instances each counter finished with its median and maximum (a median\nover all twenty with censored runs counted as above the budget, so a\nmedian is a number whenever at least ten finished), and the two\nguarantees in the common scale of the next subsection.\nFigure~\\ref{fig:practitioner} draws the same.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Random 3-CNF at $m = \\lfloor 4.26\\,n \\rceil$, twenty seeds per\nsize, one machine, 60~s per call. ``sat'' is the number of satisfiable\ninstances; ``fin'' the number a counter finished within budget; ``med''\nand ``max'' are seconds over all twenty seeds with a censored run counted\nas $> 60$. The last two columns are the brute-force and the verified\nguarantees at constant~1, $2^n$ and $2^{\\rhon}$, multiplied by the one\ncommon factor $t_{20}/2^{20}$ that puts $2^n$ through CaDiCaL's median at\n$n = 20$ ($t_{20} = T20$~s); they are shapes, not running times.\nRegenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}\n\\label{tab:practitioner}\n\\small\n\\input{tables/practitioner}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{practitioner.pdf}\n\\caption{The same data on a logarithmic time axis. Solid lines with\nmarkers are medians, dotted lines maxima, over the twenty seeds; a tool\nwhose median is censored stops at the budget line. The two guarantees are\ndrawn at constant~1 with the common factor of Table~\\ref{tab:practitioner},\nso that $2^n$ passes through the $n = 20$ measurement and $2^{\\rhon}$\ncrosses it at $n = 50$, where $\\rhon \\ge n$ begins\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). From \\repofile{paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}\n\\label{fig:practitioner}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\paragraph{The checks.} On every instance where both finished, Ganak's\ncount equals the enumeration's (ENUMAGREE instances at $n \\le 30$) and\nCaDiCaL's bit equals $[\\mathrm{count} > 0]$; ApproxMC's estimate is\nwithin its factor $1 + \\varepsilon = 1.8$ of Ganak's on WITHIN of the\nAPPROXBOTH instances where both finished (it promises that with\nprobability $1 - \\delta = 0.8$ per instance). The script exits non-zero\nif any of these fails.\n\n\\paragraph{What the solvers did.} CADICALSUMMARY GANAKSUMMARY APPROXSUMMARY\n\n\\subsection{The two guarantees beside them}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-guarantees}\n\nThe guarantees are drawn as shapes. Both are taken at constant 1 --- the\npolynomial factors, $C$, $A$ and $d$ are all set aside --- and both are\nmultiplied by the same factor $t_{20}/2^{20}$, where $t_{20}$ is\nCaDiCaL's median at $n = 20$. The factor is one number for both curves,\nso their ratio is the ratio of the guarantees themselves,\n$2^{\\rhon - n}$: below 1 for $n < 50$, equal to 1 at $n = 50$ up to the\nrounding of $\\rho(50) = 50.13$, and above 1 from there (it is RHO100 at\n$n = 100$ and RHO500 at $n = 500$). A reader who prefers each curve\nscaled to the $n = 20$ point on its own would multiply $2^{\\rhon}$ by a\nfurther $2^{20 - \\rho(20)} = 2^{2.79}$ and see the two curves touch at\n$n = 20$ and the verified one above the trivial one from there on; the\ncommon factor is the honest choice because it keeps the crossing where\nthe statement puts it (\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06a).\n\nIn that scale the brute-force guarantee reaches one second near\n$n = N1S$ and the verified guarantee near $n = N1SRHO$; at $n = 60$,\nthe largest size of the title's practical range that this section's\nsmallest tools were run at, the measured CaDiCaL median is M60 s against\n$2^n$ at G60 s and $2^{\\rhon}$ at R60 s, DEC60 and DEC60R decades\nrespectively; at $n = 500$ the decision median is M500 s against\n$10^{G500}$ s and $10^{R500}$ s. Nothing in those decades is about the\nverified machine: the gap is between what a CDCL solver does on random\nformulas and what any $2^{\\Theta(n)}$ guarantee says, and it would be\nthe same gap for the brute-force guarantee alone. The one fact this\nsection adds to the title is the order of the three objects: at every\nsize in the table the measurement is far below $2^n$, and $2^{\\rhon}$ is\nabove $2^n$ from $n = 50$ on, so the verified guarantee is the largest\nof the three at every practical size and the measurement the smallest.\n\nOne remark on what the verified machine's \\emph{running time} would have\nto be, were it ever run. Nothing in this section bears on it: the\nconstants $C, A, d$ are unknown (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), the\nmachine is a Lean term, and an existential statement is not an\nimplementation. The only values on record are the proof's own, $A \\ge\n257$ and $C \\ge 28{,}701$ (Sections~\\ref{sec:statement} and\n\\ref{sec:proof}); a guarantee of $2^{257\\,\\rhon}$ steps is a guarantee\nand not a measurement, and a different proof of the same statement could\ncarry any positive constants.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Timings on one machine, one day, named versions.} The\n  seconds are from one AMD Ryzen 9 9950X shared with other work, under\n  \\texttt{nice -n 19} with eight calls in flight, through Python\n  bindings; they are reproducible in shape and not in value, and the\n  versions are those named in Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner-setup} and\n  in \\repofile{tables/practitioner_meta.json}.\n\\item \\textbf{Random instances only.} Random 3-CNF at the threshold ratio\n  is one distribution; structured formulas can be harder for CDCL at\n  far smaller $n$ and easier for counters. Nothing is claimed about any\n  other distribution, and nothing about worst-case behaviour.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the verified machine's running time.} It is\n  unknown, in both directions; no implementation exists to run; the\n  guarantees drawn are at constant 1 and the constants are existential.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about $k > 3$.} The statement is for every fixed\n  $k$; the measurement is at $k = 3$.\n\\item \\textbf{The counters are reported, not tuned.} Ganak ran through\n  its binding at defaults, preprocessing included; ApproxMC at its\n  default tolerance; a different configuration gives different seconds.\n  The check against enumeration is at $n \\le 30$ only.\n\\item \\textbf{No statement about which tool a practitioner should use.}\n  The section shows that the verified guarantee is not binding; it does\n  not rank solvers.\n\\end{itemize}\nEOF\ntail -3 paper3/latex/tables/practitioner_run.log","description":"Draft the section with numeric placeholders; check run progress"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1019,"cache_read_input_tokens":146023,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1019,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"56304e7d-c5a9-41ea-80a7-c4a817bd6f76","uuid":"731af771-b8dd-4985-ae1a-e579a7ff59db","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:43:37.937Z","request_id":"req_011CgjAjRAmbHFX4R15Mgo7g","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01GbR3xFfkCN4KqWj6F5qJEF":{"command":"cd /home/al/dev/MOSP && cat > paper3/latex/sec7_practitioner.tex <<'EOF'\n% For the practitioner -- drafted by loop0015 item 06 (2026-11-05).\n% Every number of this section is produced by\n%   nice -n 19 python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py --regenerate   (the measurement)\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py                           (table, figure, stdout)\n% from random 3-CNF the script generates; no MOSP or pathwidth instance, nothing\n% under solutions/. Timings are from one machine on one day with named\n% versions (tables/practitioner_meta.json). The two guarantee curves are shapes\n% at constant 1, and nothing here is a running time of the verified machine.\n\\section{For the practitioner}\n\\label{sec:practitioner}\n\nThe title compares two guarantees: the verified one,\n$C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ with $C, A, d$ existential (comparator file lines\n136--139 and 210--211, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), and the trivial one,\n$2^n \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ for trying every assignment. Neither is what\na practitioner runs. What runs on a machine, for the decision bit, is a\nconflict-driven clause-learning (CDCL) solver, and for the count an exact\nor approximate model counter; and no implementation of the verified\nmachine exists to run beside them. This section measures the tools a\npractitioner has, on random 3-CNF, and draws the two guarantees next to\nthe measurements as shapes, so that the reader can see where each sits.\nThe point is small and the section says it once: the verified guarantee\nis not the binding one on any machine, because it is above $2^n$ for\nevery $n \\ge 50$ (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}) while the solvers are many\norders of magnitude below $2^n$ at every size they were run at.\n\n\\subsection{Why the comparison is between guarantees and measurements}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-why}\n\nThe verified statement is existential in the machine (comparator line\n195: \\texttt{\\textexclamdown}\\ldots\\ \\emph{see below}) and the solution's\n3,977 files contain no \\texttt{\\#eval}, no \\texttt{def main} and no\n\\texttt{IO} action (checked read-only by the script, which reads every\nfile of the family with \\texttt{open()} and counts those three patterns:\n0, 0, 0). The library proves that a machine with the stated bound\nexists and exhibits one as a Lean term inside a development with 1,048\n\\texttt{noncomputable} declarations and no executable interface\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}); nothing in the clone runs it on an input, and\nwe found no implementation elsewhere. So the verified algorithm's actual\nrunning time on any formula is unknown, in both directions: nothing says\nit is slow, and nothing says it is fast. What is known is its\n\\emph{guarantee}, and a guarantee is compared with other guarantees. The\nmeasurements below are of other programs entirely; they are in the\nsection because the question a practitioner asks is not ``which\nguarantee is smaller'' but ``what would I run'', and the answer to that\nhas not changed.\n\n\\subsection{What was run}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-setup}\n\n\\paragraph{Instances.} Random 3-CNF on $n$ variables with\n$m = \\lfloor 4.26\\,n \\rceil$ clauses, each clause three distinct variables\nwith uniform signs, generated by the script from $(n, \\mathrm{seed})$;\ntwenty seeds per size; $n = 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 80, 100, 150, 200, 250,\n300, 400, 500$. The ratio $4.26$ is the empirical satisfiability\nthreshold of random 3-SAT \\citep{Selman1996Hard}, where decision is\nhardest for a given $n$ among random formulas (the cavity method puts the\nthreshold at $4.2667$, \\citealp{Mertens2006Threshold}); the sizes are\nchosen so that the smallest ones are where the title's crossing is\n($n = 50$) and the largest are where a CDCL solver on random formulas\nbegins to take seconds. These are random instances only; nothing is\nclaimed about structured formulas.\n\n\\paragraph{Tools.} CaDiCaL 1.9.5 \\citep{Biere2024CaDiCaL} through\npython-sat 1.9.dev15 \\citep{Ignatiev2018PySAT}, one \\texttt{solve()} call,\nfor the decision bit $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$; Ganak 2.8.0\n\\citep{Sharma2019Ganak}, through the pyganak binding (which runs its Arjun\npreprocessing and then the counter), for the exact count; ApproxMC 4.4.0\n\\citep{Chakraborty2013ApproxMC, Soos2019BIRD} at its defaults\n$\\varepsilon = 0.8$, $\\delta = 0.2$ with the projection set equal to all\nvariables, for an approximate count; and, at $n \\le 30$ only, exact\ncounting by enumeration over CaDiCaL with a blocking clause per model,\nwhich is not a counter at any larger size (it is linear in the count,\nand the count is exponential in $n$) and is here as the independent check\nof the two counters where it can reach. Ganak and ApproxMC were installed\nfrom PyPI for this section; no \\texttt{sharpSAT}, \\texttt{d4} or other\ncounter binary is on the host.\n\n\\paragraph{Protocol.} Each call runs in its own interpreter under\n\\texttt{nice -n 19}, at most eight at a time on the thirty-two cores of\nan AMD Ryzen 9 9950X (Linux 7.0.0, Python 3.12.13; the machine was shared\nwith other work, load average at the start in\n\\repofile{tables/practitioner_meta.json}), with a wall-clock budget of\n60~s per call; the time recorded is \\texttt{perf\\_counter} around the\nconstruction of the solver object and the one call, inside the child,\nwith the interpreter's start-up and the import outside it. A call over\nbudget is killed and recorded as censored; a size at which a tool finishes\nfewer than ten of its twenty instances ends that tool's escalation. The\nwhole run took WALLMIN minutes.\n\n\\subsection{What came out}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-results}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:practitioner} gives, per size, the number of satisfiable\ninstances, CaDiCaL's median and maximum over the twenty seeds, the number\nof instances each counter finished with its median and maximum (a median\nover all twenty with censored runs counted as above the budget, so a\nmedian is a number whenever at least ten finished), and the two\nguarantees in the common scale of the next subsection.\nFigure~\\ref{fig:practitioner} draws the same.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Random 3-CNF at $m = \\lfloor 4.26\\,n \\rceil$, twenty seeds per\nsize, one machine, 60~s per call. ``sat'' is the number of satisfiable\ninstances; ``fin'' the number a counter finished within budget; ``med''\nand ``max'' are seconds over all twenty seeds with a censored run counted\nas $> 60$. The last two columns are the brute-force and the verified\nguarantees at constant~1, $2^n$ and $2^{\\rhon}$, multiplied by the one\ncommon factor $t_{20}/2^{20}$ that puts $2^n$ through CaDiCaL's median at\n$n = 20$ ($t_{20} = T20$~s); they are shapes, not running times.\nRegenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}\n\\label{tab:practitioner}\n\\small\n\\input{tables/practitioner}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{practitioner.pdf}\n\\caption{The same data on a logarithmic time axis. Solid lines with\nmarkers are medians, dotted lines maxima, over the twenty seeds; a tool\nwhose median is censored stops at the budget line. The two guarantees are\ndrawn at constant~1 with the common factor of Table~\\ref{tab:practitioner},\nso that $2^n$ passes through the $n = 20$ measurement and $2^{\\rhon}$\ncrosses it at $n = 50$, where $\\rhon \\ge n$ begins\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). From \\repofile{paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}\n\\label{fig:practitioner}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\paragraph{The checks.} On every instance where both finished, Ganak's\ncount equals the enumeration's (ENUMAGREE instances at $n \\le 30$) and\nCaDiCaL's bit equals $[\\mathrm{count} > 0]$; ApproxMC's estimate is\nwithin its factor $1 + \\varepsilon = 1.8$ of Ganak's on WITHIN of the\nAPPROXBOTH instances where both finished (it promises that with\nprobability $1 - \\delta = 0.8$ per instance). The script exits non-zero\nif any of these fails.\n\n\\paragraph{What the solvers did.} CADICALSUMMARY GANAKSUMMARY APPROXSUMMARY\n\n\\subsection{The two guarantees beside them}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-guarantees}\n\nThe guarantees are drawn as shapes. Both are taken at constant 1 --- the\npolynomial factors, $C$, $A$ and $d$ are all set aside --- and both are\nmultiplied by the same factor $t_{20}/2^{20}$, where $t_{20}$ is\nCaDiCaL's median at $n = 20$. The factor is one number for both curves,\nso their ratio is the ratio of the guarantees themselves,\n$2^{\\rhon - n}$: below 1 for $n < 50$, equal to 1 at $n = 50$ up to the\nrounding of $\\rho(50) = 50.13$, and above 1 from there (it is RHO100 at\n$n = 100$ and RHO500 at $n = 500$). A reader who prefers each curve\nscaled to the $n = 20$ point on its own would multiply $2^{\\rhon}$ by a\nfurther $2^{20 - \\rho(20)} = 2^{2.79}$ and see the two curves touch at\n$n = 20$ and the verified one above the trivial one from there on; the\ncommon factor is the honest choice because it keeps the crossing where\nthe statement puts it (\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06a).\n\nIn that scale the brute-force guarantee reaches one second near\n$n = N1S$ and the verified guarantee near $n = N1SRHO$; at $n = 60$,\nthe largest size of the title's practical range that this section's\nsmallest tools were run at, the measured CaDiCaL median is M60 s against\n$2^n$ at G60 s and $2^{\\rhon}$ at R60 s, DEC60 and DEC60R decades\nrespectively; at $n = 500$ the decision median is M500 s against\n$10^{G500}$ s and $10^{R500}$ s. Nothing in those decades is about the\nverified machine: the gap is between what a CDCL solver does on random\nformulas and what any $2^{\\Theta(n)}$ guarantee says, and it would be\nthe same gap for the brute-force guarantee alone. The one fact this\nsection adds to the title is the order of the three objects: at every\nsize in the table the measurement is far below $2^n$, and $2^{\\rhon}$ is\nabove $2^n$ from $n = 50$ on, so the verified guarantee is the largest\nof the three at every practical size and the measurement the smallest.\n\nOne remark on what the verified machine's \\emph{running time} would have\nto be, were it ever run. Nothing in this section bears on it: the\nconstants $C, A, d$ are unknown (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), the\nmachine is a Lean term, and an existential statement is not an\nimplementation. The only values on record are the proof's own, $A \\ge\n257$ and $C \\ge 28{,}701$ (Sections~\\ref{sec:statement} and\n\\ref{sec:proof}); a guarantee of $2^{257\\,\\rhon}$ steps is a guarantee\nand not a measurement, and a different proof of the same statement could\ncarry any positive constants.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Timings on one machine, one day, named versions.} The\n  seconds are from one AMD Ryzen 9 9950X shared with other work, under\n  \\texttt{nice -n 19} with eight calls in flight, through Python\n  bindings; they are reproducible in shape and not in value, and the\n  versions are those named in Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner-setup} and\n  in \\repofile{tables/practitioner_meta.json}.\n\\item \\textbf{Random instances only.} Random 3-CNF at the threshold ratio\n  is one distribution; structured formulas can be harder for CDCL at\n  far smaller $n$ and easier for counters. Nothing is claimed about any\n  other distribution, and nothing about worst-case behaviour.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the verified machine's running time.} It is\n  unknown, in both directions; no implementation exists to run; the\n  guarantees drawn are at constant 1 and the constants are existential.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about $k > 3$.} The statement is for every fixed\n  $k$; the measurement is at $k = 3$.\n\\item \\textbf{The counters are reported, not tuned.} Ganak ran through\n  its binding at defaults, preprocessing included; ApproxMC at its\n  default tolerance; a different configuration gives different seconds.\n  The check against enumeration is at $n \\le 30$ only.\n\\item \\textbf{No statement about which tool a practitioner should use.}\n  The section shows that the verified guarantee is not binding; it does\n  not rank solvers.\n\\end{itemize}\nEOF\ntail -3 paper3/latex/tables/practitioner_run.log","description":"Draft the section with numeric placeholders; check run progress"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01GbR3xFfkCN4KqWj6F5qJEF":{"cwd":"/home/al/dev/MOSP"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GbR3xFfkCN4KqWj6F5qJEF","type":"tool_result","content":"n= 200 cadical   finished 20/20  median    0.0578s  max    0.3906s   [   115s elapsed]\nn= 200 ganak     finished 20/20  median    2.3728s  max   29.0486s   [   115s elapsed]\nn= 200 approxmc  finished 20/20  median    0.5104s  max   25.2114s   [   115s elapsed]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"56304e7d-c5a9-41ea-80a7-c4a817bd6f76","uuid":"fa8dfa10-864f-4b9a-8e2e-3979ba9cbbf9","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:43:37.959Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"n= 200 cadical   finished 20/20  median    0.0578s  max    0.3906s   [   115s elapsed]\nn= 200 ganak     finished 20/20  median    2.3728s  max   29.0486s   [   115s elapsed]\nn= 200 approxmc  finished 20/20  median    0.5104s  max   25.2114s   [   115s elapsed]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec7_practitioner.tex","hunks":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":5,"newStart":1,"newLines":209,"lines":["-% For the practitioner -- drafted by loop0015 item 06.","+% For the practitioner -- drafted by loop0015 item 06 (2026-11-05).","+% Every number of this section is produced by","+%   nice -n 19 python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py --regenerate   (the measurement)","+%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py                           (table, figure, stdout)","+% from random 3-CNF the script generates; no MOSP or pathwidth instance, nothing","+% under solutions/. Timings are from one machine on one day with named","+% versions (tables/practitioner_meta.json). The two guarantee curves are shapes","+% at constant 1, and nothing here is a running time of the verified machine."," \\section{For the practitioner}"," \\label{sec:practitioner}"," ","-\\placeholder{Item 06 drafts this section: a SAT solver and a model counter on random 3-CNF at $n = 20\\ldots 60$ against $2^{\\rhon}$ and $2^n$ (figure labelled \\texttt{fig:practitioner}, from \\repofile{paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}); the verified guarantee is never the binding one on any machine. Ends with what this does not claim''.}","+The title compares two guarantees: the verified one,","+$C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ with $C, A, d$ existential (comparator file lines","+136--139 and 210--211, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), and the trivial one,","+$2^n \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ for trying every assignment. Neither is what","+a practitioner runs. What runs on a machine, for the decision bit, is a","+conflict-driven clause-learning (CDCL) solver, and for the count an exact","+or approximate model counter; and no implementation of the verified","+machine exists to run beside them. This section measures the tools a","+practitioner has, on random 3-CNF, and draws the two guarantees next to","+the measurements as shapes, so that the reader can see where each sits.","+The point is small and the section says it once: the verified guarantee","+is not the binding one on any machine, because it is above $2^n$ for","+every $n \\ge 50$ (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}) while the solvers are many","+orders of magnitude below $2^n$ at every size they were run at.","+","+\\subsection{Why the comparison is between guarantees and measurements}","+\\label{sec:practitioner-why}","+","+The verified statement is existential in the machine (comparator line","+195: \\texttt{\\textexclamdown}\\ldots\\ \\emph{see below}) and the solution's","+3,977 files contain no \\texttt{\\#eval}, no \\texttt{def main} and no","+\\texttt{IO} action (checked read-only by the script, which reads every","+file of the family with \\texttt{open()} and counts those three patterns:","+0, 0, 0). The library proves that a machine with the stated bound","+exists and exhibits one as a Lean term inside a development with 1,048","+\\texttt{noncomputable} declarations and no executable interface","+(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}); nothing in the clone runs it on an input, and","+we found no implementation elsewhere. So the verified algorithm's actual","+running time on any formula is unknown, in both directions: nothing says","+it is slow, and nothing says it is fast. What is known is its","+\\emph{guarantee}, and a guarantee is compared with other guarantees. The","+measurements below are of other programs entirely; they are in the","+section because the question a practitioner asks is not ``which","+guarantee is smaller'' but ``what would I run'', and the answer to that","+has not changed.","+","+\\subsection{What was run}","+\\label{sec:practitioner-setup}","+","+\\paragraph{Instances.} Random 3-CNF on $n$ variables with","+$m = \\lfloor 4.26\\,n \\rceil$ clauses, each clause three distinct variables","+with uniform signs, generated by the script from $(n, \\mathrm{seed})$;","+twenty seeds per size; $n = 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 80, 100, 150, 200, 250,","+300, 400, 500$. The ratio $4.26$ is the empirical satisfiability","+threshold of random 3-SAT \\citep{Selman1996Hard}, where decision is","+hardest for a given $n$ among random formulas (the cavity method puts the","+threshold at $4.2667$, \\citealp{Mertens2006Threshold}); the sizes are","+chosen so that the smallest ones are where the title's crossing is","+($n = 50$) and the largest are where a CDCL solver on random formulas","+begins to take seconds. These are random instances only; nothing is","+claimed about structured formulas.","+","+\\paragraph{Tools.} CaDiCaL 1.9.5 \\citep{Biere2024CaDiCaL} through","+python-sat 1.9.dev15 \\citep{Ignatiev2018PySAT}, one \\texttt{solve()} call,","+for the decision bit $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$; Ganak 2.8.0","+\\citep{Sharma2019Ganak}, through the pyganak binding (which runs its Arjun","+preprocessing and then the counter), for the exact count; ApproxMC 4.4.0","+\\citep{Chakraborty2013ApproxMC, Soos2019BIRD} at its defaults","+$\\varepsilon = 0.8$, $\\delta = 0.2$ with the projection set equal to all","+variables, for an approximate count; and, at $n \\le 30$ only, exact","+counting by enumeration over CaDiCaL with a blocking clause per model,","+which is not a counter at any larger size (it is linear in the count,","+and the count is exponential in $n$) and is here as the independent check","+of the two counters where it can reach. Ganak and ApproxMC were installed","+from PyPI for this section; no \\texttt{sharpSAT}, \\texttt{d4} or other","+counter binary is on the host.","+","+\\paragraph{Protocol.} Each call runs in its own interpreter under","+\\texttt{nice -n 19}, at most eight at a time on the thirty-two cores of","+an AMD Ryzen 9 9950X (Linux 7.0.0, Python 3.12.13; the machine was shared","+with other work, load average at the start in","+\\repofile{tables/practitioner_meta.json}), with a wall-clock budget of","+60~s per call; the time recorded is \\texttt{perf\\_counter} around the","+construction of the solver object and the one call, inside the child,","+with the interpreter's start-up and the import outside it. A call over","+budget is killed and recorded as censored; a size at which a tool finishes","+fewer than ten of its twenty instances ends that tool's escalation. The","+whole run took WALLMIN minutes.","+","+\\subsection{What came out}","+\\label{sec:practitioner-results}","+","+Table~\\ref{tab:practitioner} gives, per size, the number of satisfiable","+instances, CaDiCaL's median and maximum over the twenty seeds, the number","+of instances each counter finished with its median and maximum (a median","+over all twenty with censored runs counted as above the budget, so a","+median is a number whenever at least ten finished), and the two","+guarantees in the common scale of the next subsection.","+Figure~\\ref{fig:practitioner} draws the same.","+","+\\begin{table}[htbp]","+\\centering","+\\caption{Random 3-CNF at $m = \\lfloor 4.26\\,n \\rceil$, twenty seeds per","+size, one machine, 60~s per call. ``sat'' is the number of satisfiable","+instances; ``fin'' the number a counter finished within budget; ``med''","+and ``max'' are seconds over all twenty seeds with a censored run counted","+as $> 60$. The last two columns are the brute-force and the verified","+guarantees at constant~1, $2^n$ and $2^{\\rhon}$, multiplied by the one","+common factor $t_{20}/2^{20}$ that puts $2^n$ through CaDiCaL's median at","+$n = 20$ ($t_{20} = T20$~s); they are shapes, not running times.","+Regenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}","+\\label{tab:practitioner}","+\\small","+\\input{tables/practitioner}","+\\end{table}","+","+\\begin{figure}[htbp]","+\\centering","+\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{practitioner.pdf}","+\\caption{The same data on a logarithmic time axis. Solid lines with","+markers are medians, dotted lines maxima, over the twenty seeds; a tool","+whose median is censored stops at the budget line. The two guarantees are","+drawn at constant~1 with the common factor of Table~\\ref{tab:practitioner},","+so that $2^n$ passes through the $n = 20$ measurement and $2^{\\rhon}$","+crosses it at $n = 50$, where $\\rhon \\ge n$ begins","+(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). From \\repofile{paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}","+\\label{fig:practitioner}","+\\end{figure}","+","+\\paragraph{The checks.} On every instance where both finished, Ganak's","+count equals the enumeration's (ENUMAGREE instances at $n \\le 30$) and","+CaDiCaL's bit equals $[\\mathrm{count} > 0]$; ApproxMC's estimate is","+within its factor $1 + \\varepsilon = 1.8$ of Ganak's on WITHIN of the","+APPROXBOTH instances where both finished (it promises that with","+probability $1 - \\delta = 0.8$ per instance). The script exits non-zero","+if any of these fails.","+","+\\paragraph{What the solvers did.} CADICALSUMMARY GANAKSUMMARY APPROXSUMMARY","+","+\\subsection{The two guarantees beside them}","+\\label{sec:practitioner-guarantees}","+","+The guarantees are drawn as shapes. Both are taken at constant 1 --- the","+polynomial factors, $C$, $A$ and $d$ are all set aside --- and both are","+multiplied by the same factor $t_{20}/2^{20}$, where $t_{20}$ is","+CaDiCaL's median at $n = 20$. The factor is one number for both curves,","+so their ratio is the ratio of the guarantees themselves,","+$2^{\\rhon - n}$: below 1 for $n < 50$, equal to 1 at $n = 50$ up to the","+rounding of $\\rho(50) = 50.13$, and above 1 from there (it is RHO100 at","+$n = 100$ and RHO500 at $n = 500$). A reader who prefers each curve","+scaled to the $n = 20$ point on its own would multiply $2^{\\rhon}$ by a","+further $2^{20 - \\rho(20)} = 2^{2.79}$ and see the two curves touch at","+$n = 20$ and the verified one above the trivial one from there on; the","+common factor is the honest choice because it keeps the crossing where","+the statement puts it (\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06a).","+","+In that scale the brute-force guarantee reaches one second near","+$n = N1S$ and the verified guarantee near $n = N1SRHO$; at $n = 60$,","+the largest size of the title's practical range that this section's","+smallest tools were run at, the measured CaDiCaL median is M60 s against","+$2^n$ at G60 s and $2^{\\rhon}$ at R60 s, DEC60 and DEC60R decades","+respectively; at $n = 500$ the decision median is M500 s against","+$10^{G500}$ s and $10^{R500}$ s. Nothing in those decades is about the","+verified machine: the gap is between what a CDCL solver does on random","+formulas and what any $2^{\\Theta(n)}$ guarantee says, and it would be","+the same gap for the brute-force guarantee alone. The one fact this","+section adds to the title is the order of the three objects: at every","+size in the table the measurement is far below $2^n$, and $2^{\\rhon}$ is","+above $2^n$ from $n = 50$ on, so the verified guarantee is the largest","+of the three at every practical size and the measurement the smallest.","+","+One remark on what the verified machine's \\emph{running time} would have","+to be, were it ever run. Nothing in this section bears on it: the","+constants $C, A, d$ are unknown (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), the","+machine is a Lean term, and an existential statement is not an","+implementation. The only values on record are the proof's own, $A \\ge","+257$ and $C \\ge 28{,}701$ (Sections~\\ref{sec:statement} and","+\\ref{sec:proof}); a guarantee of $2^{257\\,\\rhon}$ steps is a guarantee","+and not a measurement, and a different proof of the same statement could","+carry any positive constants.","+","+\\subsection{What this does not claim}","+\\label{sec:practitioner-not}","+","+\\begin{itemize}","+\\item \\textbf{Timings on one machine, one day, named versions.} The","+  seconds are from one AMD Ryzen 9 9950X shared with other work, under","+  \\texttt{nice -n 19} with eight calls in flight, through Python","+  bindings; they are reproducible in shape and not in value, and the","+  versions are those named in Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner-setup} and","+  in \\repofile{tables/practitioner_meta.json}.","+\\item \\textbf{Random instances only.} Random 3-CNF at the threshold ratio","+  is one distribution; structured formulas can be harder for CDCL at","+  far smaller $n$ and easier for counters. Nothing is claimed about any","+  other distribution, and nothing about worst-case behaviour.","+\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the verified machine's running time.} It is","+  unknown, in both directions; no implementation exists to run; the","+  guarantees drawn are at constant 1 and the constants are existential.","+\\item \\textbf{Nothing about $k > 3$.} The statement is for every fixed","+  $k$; the measurement is at $k = 3$.","+\\item \\textbf{The counters are reported, not tuned.} Ganak ran through","+  its binding at defaults, preprocessing included; ApproxMC at its","+  default tolerance; a different configuration gives different seconds.","+  The check against enumeration is at $n \\le 30$ only.","+\\item \\textbf{No statement about which tool a practitioner should use.}","+  The section shows that the verified guarantee is not binding; it does","+  not rank solvers.","+\\end{itemize}"]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec7_practitioner.tex"]}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01GbR3xFfkCN4KqWj6F5qJEF","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
